Run `M13_test_time_compute_experiments.py`. It contains three experiments:


1. parity: a linear readout from $X$ versus a readout from a sequential working state;

2. voting under independent and correlated errors;

3. best-of-$N$ under a good and a weak verifier.

Explain:

- why the scratchpad makes the answer linearly accessible even though $I(Y;C\mid X)=0$;
- why correlation reduces the return from additional samples;
- why oracle pass@N can rise while selected accuracy falls.
